import{_ as n,q as o,o as s,c as r,b as t,k as i,j as h,m as d}from"./chunks/framework.D3-xQjZb.js";const y=JSON.parse('{"title":"What Could You Learn If You Could Listen to Every Student Work?","description":"Turn an assignment into an AI Studio, collect student explanations as they work, and use AI-assisted grading to plan individual feedback and the next class.","frontmatter":{"title":"What Could You Learn If You Could Listen to Every Student Work?","description":"Turn an assignment into an AI Studio, collect student explanations as they work, and use AI-assisted grading to plan individual feedback and the next class.","teaser":"Give an AI coach an assignment you already teach. What students say while working can help you grade one project and decide what to teach the whole class tomorrow.","articleGenre":"Classroom feature","imageCaption":"A teacher can design the question and decide which responses are worth bringing back to class.","socialImage":"/images/editorial/social-field-desk.jpg","socialImageAlt":"An open notebook with hand-drawn diagrams and a red pencil on a blue desk.","socialImageWidth":1200,"socialImageHeight":630},"headers":[],"relativePath":"teaching/when-ai-helps-write-the-code.md","filePath":"teaching/when-ai-helps-write-the-code.md"}'),c={name:"teaching/when-ai-helps-write-the-code.md"};function u(l,e,p,g,w,m){const a=o("ArticleHeader");return s(),r("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t("h1",{id:"what-could-you-learn-if-you-could-listen-to-every-student-work",tabindex:"-1"},[i("What Could You Learn If You Could Listen to Every Student Work? "),t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#what-could-you-learn-if-you-could-listen-to-every-student-work","aria-label":'Permalink to "What Could You Learn If You Could Listen to Every Student Work?"'},"​")],-1)),h(a),e[1]||(e[1]=d('<p>One of the best reasons to pull up a chair beside a student is to hear the story behind a choice. Imagine a student pointing to a size chart on a website they built. “I put the measurements at the top. Then I tried to buy the shirt and realized the size button took me right past them.” Now I have something to teach with. I know what the student expected, what surprised them, and why they changed the page. While I’m helping one student, I miss exchanges like that at the next table.</p><p>That’s why I make assignments I call <em>Studios</em>. I can take something I already teach, give an AI coach a few good questions to ask while the student works, and decide which responses belong in the hand-in. When I sit down to grade, I can use AI to read that evidence with me at two levels. What feedback would help this particular student? What should I do with the whole class tomorrow?</p><h2 id="start-with-an-assignment-you-already-teach" tabindex="-1">Start with an assignment you already teach <a class="header-anchor" href="#start-with-an-assignment-you-already-teach" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Start with an assignment you already teach&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Open an ordinary AI chat, paste in the teacher guide and an assignment, and type <strong>“Help me turn this into a Studio.”</strong> The guide asks which decision you want students to learn to make. It drafts a short lesson for them and instructions for a coach that can ask a question, offer a hint, and wait for the reply. You can use your existing rubric and submission process. <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/kaw393939.github.io/blob/main/examples/ai-studio-starter/TEACHER-STUDIO.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the teacher guide</a></p><p>I’d try the lesson once before handing it out. Play a student and say, “I’m stuck. Pick for me.” If the coach makes the very decision you wanted the student to practice, you’ve found a line to edit. Ask it to offer an example from a different problem, then try the exchange again. The template gives you a quick way to rehearse an assignment from the other side of the desk.</p><h2 id="decide-what-you-want-to-hear" tabindex="-1">Decide what you want to hear <a class="header-anchor" href="#decide-what-you-want-to-hear" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decide what you want to hear&quot;">​</a></h2><p>I design these assignments around <strong>Research → Plan → Build → Test → Explain</strong>. Each stage gives the coach a reason to ask a different question. In the Knowledge Lab, a student building an app that answers questions from documents can begin by reading a source and finding a question it can answer. During planning, the coach might ask, “Which passage do you expect your app to find?” After the student builds and tests the app, the coach can return to that prediction. “What did it actually return? Show me the words that support your answer.” The student can set the passages side by side and explain what they would keep or change. <a href="/knowledge-lab/students/research" target="_self">Try the Knowledge Lab research step</a>.</p><p>You choose how much of that conversation to collect. The general starter lets a learner submit a short exchange, annotate the work, or explain a choice aloud. My Brand Studio gives a more structured example. Its coach drafts a learning record with the student’s words or a labeled paraphrase, the help they received, and a link to the work or check they’re discussing. The student can correct the record before submitting it with their work. A brief answer like “yes” stays brief. The coach leaves the reasoning open for the student to supply. <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/is117-brand-studio/blob/main/LEARNING.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">See the Brand Studio learning record</a></p><h2 id="grade-one-project-then-read-across-the-class" tabindex="-1">Grade one project, then read across the class <a class="header-anchor" href="#grade-one-project-then-read-across-the-class" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Grade one project, then read across the class&quot;">​</a></h2><p>For an individual review, I can give AI the rubric, the project, and the responses the student chose to submit. I’d start with a request like this.</p><blockquote><p>Find a choice this student can explain. Show me their words and the part of the project they refer to. Draft feedback that recognizes what they did well and asks one question the evidence leaves open.</p></blockquote><p>Take the student who moved the size chart. The site shows where the measurements ended up. Their explanation gives me a reason to credit the decision to revise. That frees me to ask, <em>How would you check whether someone buying their first shirt notices the measurements now?</em> I review the evidence and decide the grade. <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/is117-brand-studio/blob/main/docs/assessment.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">See the instructor’s assessment guide</a></p><p>Then I can ask something that grading one project at a time makes easy to miss.</p><blockquote><p>Across these submissions, where did students give different reasons for the same choice? Find a difficulty worth teaching again and an approach another student could learn from. Point me to the responses behind each observation.</p></blockquote><p>Suppose several students say their search worked because it returned a passage. One student opens the original document and discovers that the next sentence changes the answer. I now have a way to open our next class. Give everyone that passage and ask what advice they would give the reader before and after seeing the next sentence. I can also turn the question back on my assignment. If the coach told everyone exactly where to look, I’ll want to leave that decision open on the next case. The starter supplies the lesson and coach. The instructor runs the review using the submitted work.</p><p>If you try this, start with a question you already ask when you pull up a chair beside a student. Build it into an assignment. When you review the replies, look for one that changes the feedback you give that student and one that changes what you teach next. <a href="/downloads/AI-Studio-Starter.zip">Download the teacher Studio, including worked answers</a> · <a href="/teaching/studio-field-notes">Read the teaching field notes</a></p>',16))])}const k=n(c,[["render",u]]);export{y as __pageData,k as default};
